facts:
A **broken (truncated) axis** that does not start at $0$ makes small differences look huge.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large},scale=0.9]
\draw[->] (0,0)--(0,6.0); \draw[->] (0,0)--(6.0,0);
\foreach \y/\lab in {0/68,1.5/70,3/72,4.5/74} {\draw (-0.15,\y)--(0,\y); \node[left,font=\small] at (-0.15,\y) {$\lab$};}
\draw (1.0,0) rectangle (2.2,1.5); \draw (3.2,0) rectangle (4.4,5.25);
\node[below,font=\small] at (1.6,0) {P}; \node[below,font=\small] at (3.8,0) {Q};
\node[rotate=90] at (-1.7,2.5) {Value};
\node[below] at (3.0,-1.0) {Item};
\node[font=\small] at (3.0,6.6) {axis starts at $68$: gap looks huge};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

The same two values on an axis starting at $0$ show the true, much smaller gap.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large},scale=0.9]
\draw[->] (0,0)--(0,6.0); \draw[->] (0,0)--(6.0,0);
\foreach \y/\lab in {0/0,1.1/20,2.2/40,3.3/60,4.4/80} {\draw (-0.15,\y)--(0,\y); \node[left,font=\small] at (-0.15,\y) {$\lab$};}
\draw (1.0,0) rectangle (2.2,3.85); \draw (3.2,0) rectangle (4.4,4.125);
\node[below,font=\small] at (1.6,0) {P}; \node[below,font=\small] at (3.8,0) {Q};
\node[rotate=90] at (-1.7,2.5) {Value};
\node[below] at (3.0,-1.0) {Item};
\node[font=\small] at (3.0,6.6) {axis starts at $0$: same data, small gap};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

foundation.question_text:
A café advert uses this graph to compare cups sold.
What feature makes the difference look much larger than it is?
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large},scale=0.9]
\draw[->] (0,0)--(0,6.4); \draw[->] (0,0)--(6.5,0); \foreach \y/\lab in {0/95,2.5/100,5/105} {\draw (-0.15,\y)--(0,\y); \node[left,font=\scriptsize] at (-0.15,\y) {$\lab$};}
\draw (1.1,0) rectangle (2.5,1.5); \draw (3.5,0) rectangle (4.9,3.5); \node[below,font=\scriptsize] at (1.8,0) {Mon}; \node[below,font=\scriptsize] at (4.2,0) {Tue}; \node[rotate=90] at (-1.4,2.5) {Cups sold}; \node[below] at (3,-1.0) {Day}; \node at (3,6.1) {Coffee sales};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

What is misleading about the scale on this café graph?
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large},scale=0.9]
\draw[->] (0,0)--(0,5.4); \draw[->] (0,0)--(6.5,0); \foreach \y/\lab in {0/0,1/10,2/20,4/30} {\draw (-0.15,\y)--(0,\y); \node[left,font=\scriptsize] at (-0.15,\y) {$\lab$};}
\draw (1.1,0) rectangle (2.5,2); \draw (3.5,0) rectangle (4.9,4); \node[below,font=\scriptsize] at (1.8,0) {Tea}; \node[below,font=\scriptsize] at (4.2,0) {Juice}; \node[rotate=90] at (-1.2,2) {Orders}; \node[below] at (3,-1.0) {Drink}; \node at (3,5.1) {Lunch orders};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

The bars show the correct heights, but the display is still unfair.
What is the problem?
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large},scale=0.9]
\draw[->] (0,0)--(0,7.4); \draw[->] (0,0)--(7.5,0); \foreach \y in {0,2,4,6} {\draw (-0.15,\y)--(0,\y); \node[left,font=\scriptsize] at (-0.15,\y) {$\y$};}
\draw (0.8,0) rectangle (1.6,5); \draw (2.8,0) rectangle (6.3,5); \node[below,font=\scriptsize] at (1.2,0) {Muffin}; \node[below,font=\scriptsize] at (4.55,0) {Scone}; \node[rotate=90] at (-1.2,3) {Dozens sold}; \node[below] at (3.5,-1.0) {Item}; \node at (3.5,7.1) {Bakery sales};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

